  2025 Margaux Report
  
  Like the rest of Bordeaux, the growing season for Margaux began with an unseasonably warm, dry winter and spring.
  
  The appellation also encountered the searing heatwaves of July and August which saw temperatures rise. It is likely that older Cabernet Sauvignon vines with deeper roots fared better than the younger vines that may have struggled in the heat.
  
  As a result of the heat, the harvest was earlier than usual with the first white varieties brought in mid-August, and the last of the Cabernet Sauvignon brought in early October.
  
  Although time will be the great revealer, it is likely that a wealth of rich age-worthy reds will come out of Margaux. Whether it stands to be a good or great vintage is yet to be established, however, there are likely some very good wines to be had.
  
